export const LOWTIDE_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"LOWTIDE\"\n\nBuild a landing page for **LOWTIDE**, a fictional titanium sleep ring whose app has no feed and no notifications (\"the anti-doom-scroll ring\"). It must not feel like a landing page: it is one full-screen three.js night on a moonlit tidal flat. Sleep is a tide that goes out and comes back about five times a night, so **scrolling is the clock (23:04 → 07:00)** and each low tide uncovers one part of the story.\n\n## Stack\n\nNext.js 16 App Router (own route group + root layout), React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind v4. `npm i three gsap lenis troika-three-text tone`. Use `THREE.WebGLRenderer` (three's `Water`, `GPUComputationRenderer` and troika are WebGL-only).\n\n## Story (one chapter per tide)\n\n1. **23:04, high tide (hook):** \"Let the tide go out.\" — quiet line \"A sleep ring for people who can't put the phone down.\"\n2. **00:40 (problem):** \"The scroll doesn't end. The night does.\" Bioluminescent plankton (GPU particles, ~37k) swarm into sourced figures: **1 in 3** US adults sleep under seven hours (CDC MMWR 2016), **7h** minimum (CDC), **95%** use a screen in the hour before bed (National Sleep Foundation 2011).\n3. **02:15 (how it works):** \"Three pools, read all night.\" Three rock pools: heart rate (rings at 60 bpm), skin temperature (water warms/cools over 8 s), breathing (a rim swells 12×/min).\n4. **03:50 (smart wake):** \"We wake you on the outgoing tide.\" A post-process ink melt bleeds the frame while arriving and resolves at rest.\n5. **05:25 (proof):** three testimonials beside footprint trails pressed in wet sand; after the low tide the water returns and washes each trail away.\n6. **06:40 (signature):** \"The moon, on your hand.\" The moon's reflection in the last pool ripples, bends into a ring outline, and a procedural titanium ring (lathe of a rounded-rectangle profile, `MeshPhysicalMaterial`, `RoomEnvironment`) rises out of the water. Specs and \"$279 · no subscription\" etched in the sand.\n7. **07:00 dawn (conversion):** \"Your night\": bedtime + wake time → a glowing tide line over the water (one dip per 90-min cycle, smart-wake marker), drag/arrow keys to walk it, \"Keep your night\" downloads a PNG; pre-order form (email, ring size 6–13, finish Moonstone / Tidewater / Driftwood) with a tide-wash success \"See you at low tide.\"\n\nProduct facts: grade 5 titanium, 7-day battery, smart-wake window 10–30 min, $279, no subscription.\n\n## Interaction vocabulary (no gates)\n\n- **Lantern cursor:** a screen-space warm pool of light; headlines appear only where it shines (faint ghost elsewhere).\n- **Stillness:** 1.2 s without pointer/scroll movement → calm rises over 2 s; quiet lines surface from below.\n- **Speed-reactive water:** smoothed |scroll velocity| → roughness: distortion, foam, torn text, scattered plankton, harsher sound.\n- **Settle:** 600 ms after input stops, ease to the nearest chapter; new input cancels it.\n- **Moon dial** (bottom right, `role=\"slider\"`): drag to scrub, notches jump to chapters, arrows/Page keys step; Space/arrows on the page step chapters unless a control has focus.\n- **Sound** (off by default): Tone.js — brown-noise surf through a low-pass that follows tide and roughness, grit, a slow swell, a sine pad that slows through the night, a chime when a low tide settles.\n\n## World\n\nShore 360 units long along x, camera walks it with progress; sand ramps up 0.04/unit toward the camera; water level −2.1 (low) → 0.7 (high) from `tideLevel(p)` (half-cosine between anchors). Wet sand stays dark after the water leaves and mirrors the moon in a glittering streak. Low moon (NASA CGI Moon Kit texture), gradient sky dome with hashed stars, three procedural headland ridgelines. Bloom + grain + vignette.\n\n## Palette & type\n\nnight `#0A0D16`, foam `#ECEFF0`, moonlight `#EBD9A6` (only accent), plankton `#8FD3FF`, dusk `#7A8494`, dawn `#F2B28C`. Display Imbue, body Onest, utility Xanh Mono (italic for quiet lines).\n\n## Accessibility & fallback\n\nRender every word server-side as a finished \"tide table\" page (one h1, an h2 per low tide, stats, pools, quotes, specs, a night calculator and the pre-order form). Reduced motion, no JS, no WebGL2 or context loss → that page, no canvas. In experience mode it is visually hidden (controls inside it `inert`) and an `aria-live` region announces each settled chapter.\n\n## Quality bar\n\nPure, tested modules for clock, tide, chapter weights, roughness/calm, night plan, validation, dial geometry and glyph sampling; Playwright for the dial, keyboard, reload restore, settle-cancel, finale download and pre-order, and the reduced-motion tide table. No performance or responsiveness budget.\n";

export const STARLIGHT_PROMPT = `# Prompt: rebuild Starlight Lab

Build a Next.js App Router landing page for fictional Starlight Lab, a pure WebGL2 model study space. Use Manrope with Geist Mono for utility text. The hero contains a live planet rendered directly with the browser WebGL2 API. Do not import Three.js. Compile a vertex and fragment shader, draw a full screen triangle, animate with requestAnimationFrame and update a pointer uniform. Include benefits, process cards, proof stats, FAQ disclosures and a local starter form. Use IntersectionObserver for section reveals and respect reduced motion.
`;

export const FIRSTDAY_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"FIRSTDAY\"\n\nBuild a complete fictional learning-by-doing landing page with Next.js App Router, React, TypeScript, Tailwind v4 and Lenis. Install `next react react-dom lenis`. Route `/firstday` has its own root layout and imports only from its own route group. Reference learnwithzero.com for pale architectural scale, centered display typography and scroll storytelling. Create original branding, illustrations and copy.\n\nThe central idea: experience opens doors, so a miniature campus leads into your first project as you scroll. FIRSTDAY's voice is calm, inviting, specific and optimistic. Headline: “Big futures. Small first steps.” Tagline: “A little experience changes everything.” Create an SVG doorway/sun mark and lowercase firstday. lockup.\n\nPalette: paper #F5F6F2, graphite #242B29, sage #D3E6D5, moss #778F80, chalk #FFFFFF, stone #E9E9E3, periwinkle #CAD4F1, apricot #F1C7A8. Tints use color-mix. Text uses graphite. Use Manrope for display, DM Sans for body and IBM Plex Mono for labels, all self-hosted through next/font/google with distinct CSS variables.\n\nBuild: fixed responsive header; centered two-line hero with masked text, short supporting copy and sage pill CTA; original chalk-white isometric campus artwork with circular forum, studios, trees and map pins; fictional partner wordmarks; six filterable learning-path cards; native project dialog showing an actual sample brief; three-stage sticky workspace explaining try, collaborate, and portfolio; three fictional learner-story cards; four native FAQ disclosures; a local-only signup demo; oversized lowercase footer wordmark.\n\nUse an original architectural SVG rather than downloading the reference's model. Save the SVG and locally rasterize it, then process through the repo image pipeline into 800/1600 AVIF and WebP with a typed manifest. Hero image gets high fetch priority; all images have intrinsic sizes. Project illustrations are inline SVG blocks, rings, bars, nodes, a target and an open book.\n\nMotion personality: tactile and optimistic. Verbs gather and arrive. Signature moment: the campus gently dollies closer, then a first-project brief becomes a sticky workspace and changes through three stages as the visitor scrolls. Use real measured section offsets and one rAF coordinator. Never set React state per scroll frame. A stage button allows direct access; manual selection survives until a scroll stage boundary. Native scrolling on touch. Load Lenis only for fine pointers and no reduced-motion preference. Header compresses with a page-progress line. Reveal vocabulary: headline mask, scale-rise card cascade, clip wipe and drawn rule. Cards lift, artwork scales, CTA fill wipes, arrows move, presses scale to .97, and forms transition through loading and a named local success receipt. Ambient note drift pauses offscreen, in hidden tabs and through an explicit pause button. Easing out cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1), expo cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1), in-out cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1), spring linear(0,.5 15%,1.03 50%,1). Durations 90/180/420/850/1200ms; stagger 70ms.\n\nAt 390px, use a compact native-details nav, two-column role cards, a horizontally scrollable learner-story row, a single-column form and a normal-flow three-stage workspace. Reduced motion shows all scenes with no animation, no Lenis and no hidden reveals. No-JavaScript renders the full story and all role cards. Native links still reach the signup. Use one h1, semantic landmarks, visible focus rings, named controls, escaped user strings and allowlisted project IDs. Signup validates name/email, never sends a request and explicitly explains that no enrollment or email was created. All brands, learner quotes and network names are fictional.\n\nKeep source divided into brand, campus, projects, journey, motion, forms and sections. Add DESIGN.md, favicon, social image, media direction, gallery metadata and preview, unit tests for progress/filtering/validation, and Playwright tests for filters, dialog, scroll stages, signup, mobile, reduced motion and no JavaScript. Verify palette isolation, types, unit tests and browser behavior. Never deploy or commit automatically.\n";

export const ORRERY_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"Orrery\" (presentation-style landing page)\n\nPaste everything below the line into your AI coding agent.\n\n---\n\nBuild a landing page in **Next.js (App Router) + React + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4** for a fictional AI company called **Orrery**. It's \"the orchestration layer for teams of AI agents\": it coordinates specialised agents the way a clockwork orrery moves its planets.\n\nThe page must work as a **10-slide keynote**, and look editorial and hand-crafted, never like a component kit or a generic AI site.\n\n**Reference images** (look and feel only; generate your own): https://openlandingpage.vercel.app/templates/orrery/title-orbits-1600.webp, …/problem-single-1600.webp, …/usecase-1-1600.webp, …/security-texture-1600.webp. The live template is at https://openlandingpage.vercel.app/orrery.\n\n## Brand\n\n- **Palette, four colours only:**\n  - ink `#0E0F13`\n  - bone `#F2EFE8` (the page background)\n  - ultramarine `#2F3BFF` (the single accent)\n  - amber `#FFB000` (rare secondary)\n\n  Make tints with `color-mix()` of these. Never introduce other hues, gradients, glows, purple, robots, brains or circuit imagery.\n- **Type (via `next/font/google`):**\n  - **Instrument Serif** 400 for display and headings. Every headline ends in an *italic turn*, for example \"Many agents, *one clockwork.*\"\n  - **Inter Tight** for body text, 17px / 1.55.\n  - **Geist Mono** 12px uppercase with 0.08em tracking for labels, the counter, buttons and eyebrows.\n- **Logo:** the word \"Orrery.\" in Instrument Serif with the full stop in ultramarine.\n- **Icon:** a dot inside two concentric orbit rings, with one ultramarine satellite at the top right, on a bone rounded square.\n- **Layout:**\n  - Gutter `clamp(20px, 5vw, 80px)`, 12-column grid with 24px gaps.\n  - Each slide opens with a mono eyebrow: a 28px rule, then \"03 — The orrery\".\n  - Buttons are 44px pills: outline, or solid ultramarine.\n\n## Keynote mechanics (desktop ≥ 900px without reduced motion)\n\n- The page doesn't scroll. All slides are absolutely stacked at full viewport size.\n- Each slide gets `data-state` = `past` / `active` / `future`:\n  - **future:** `clip-path: inset(100% 0 0 0)`, on top.\n  - **active:** `inset(0)`.\n  - **past:** `inset(0)` plus `translateY(-7vh)`, underneath.\n  - Transition clip-path and transform over 700ms `cubic-bezier(0.77, 0, 0.175, 1)`, so the next slide wipes up over the previous one.\n- **Input:**\n  - Wheel and touch go through GSAP `Observer` with `wheelSpeed: -1`, `onUp` → next, `onDown` → previous, and a 650ms lock per step.\n  - Keys: ↓ → PageDown Space go forward, ↑ ← PageUp go back, Home/End jump to the ends. **Ignore keys whose target is an input, textarea, select or contenteditable.**\n- **Chrome:**\n  - A fixed slide counter \"03 / 10\" at bottom left (mono pill).\n  - A chapter rail at right: one button per slide, shown as a short rule. The active one has a long rule, and labels reveal on hover.\n  - A fixed top bar with the logo and a \"Book a demo\" button that links to `#cta`.\n  - The bar, counter and rail switch colours with the active slide's tone.\n- **Deep links:** each slide has an id. The hash updates on change and is read on load. Disable transitions until after that first jump.\n- **Mode detection:** start in an \"unknown\" mode, then use `matchMedia('(min-width: 900px) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)')`. Only attach keynote listeners or flow-mode observers once the mode is known.\n- **Content build:** inside the active slide, children fade and rise 18px, staggered `index × 80ms + 280ms`.\n\n**On phones and with reduced motion:** render the slides as normal stacked full-height sections with ordinary scrolling. The counter follows an IntersectionObserver. Nothing waits on an animation to become visible.\n\n## The 10 slides (bone background unless marked ink)\n\n1. **Title:**\n   - Mono kicker \"The orchestration layer for AI agents\" in ultramarine.\n   - Display h1 \"Many agents, / *one clockwork.*\"\n   - One-sentence lead, buttons \"Book a demo\" (solid) and \"How it works →\".\n   - A brass-orrery photo on the right 5 columns.\n   - Hint text \"Scroll, or press →\".\n2. **Problem:** a portrait photo of a lone brass sphere at left, then the h2 \"One agent *isn't* a team.\" and three numbered failures: \"It loses the thread.\" / \"It does everything at one depth.\" / \"Nobody checks its work.\"\n3. **The orrery (ink):**\n   - h2 \"Specialists on their own orbits, *kept in time.*\" with a 5-row legend: The conductor, Researchers, Builders, Shared memory, The hand-off (in amber).\n   - An SVG diagram in a 600×600 viewBox that builds every 450ms while active:\n     - core\n     - inner orbit with 2 bone agents\n     - outer dashed orbit with 3 agents, one large and ultramarine\n     - thin links from the core to every agent\n     - an amber dot travelling core → inner agent → ultramarine agent (`animateMotion`)\n   - Each legend row lights up as its step appears. Under reduced motion it's fully built.\n4. **How it works:** h2 \"Three steps from brief *to finished work.*\" Then three columns, each with a big ultramarine serif numeral, an h3 and a line of text: Describe the work / Orrery assembles the team / Review, approve, ship.\n5. **Use cases:** h2 \"Where teams put *it to work.*\" Three cards (a 4:3 image, an h3, text): Customer operations / Research and analysis / Software engineering.\n6. **Results:** h2 \"Measured in work *that ships.*\" Three huge serif figures with ultramarine units that count up when the slide is active: 4.2×, 63%, 99.6%. Short labels and a mono footnote.\n7. **Customers:** a large italic serif pull quote over a faint ink-wash circle texture, plus two smaller quotes with names and roles.\n8. **Pricing:** h2 \"Priced by the team, *not the token.*\" Three tiers: Starter $49, Team $390 (ink-filled, amber \"Most teams\" badge) and Enterprise Custom. Each has check-mark features.\n9. **Security (ink):** h2 \"Built for teams that *answer to auditors.*\" A 2×3 grid of claims (SOC 2 Type II, data residency, every hand-off logged, approval gates, no training on your work, SSO and SCIM) and a guilloché-texture image column.\n10. **Book a demo:**\n    - Display \"Put your agents *in orbit.*\" and a lead paragraph.\n    - A form card with Name, Work email and Company. Validate client-side with these errors: \"Add your name\" / \"Enter a work email\" / \"Add your company\".\n    - On success, show \"Thanks — we'll be in touch within one working day.\" **Never send a network request.**\n    - Footer row with the mark, \"© 2026 Orrery Labs\" and links.\n\n## Images\n\nGenerate 8 editorial still-life images. The style: warm bone paper, near-black ink, small ultramarine and amber accents, brass, glass and paper, soft daylight, lots of negative space. No people, screens, robots or text.\n\nKeys: `title-orbits` (16:9), `problem-single` (4:5), `usecase-1..3` (4:3), `quote-texture` (1:1), `security-texture` (16:9), `og-base` (16:9).\n\nUntil an image exists, render a hatched placeholder box labelled with its key, so the layout never breaks.\n\n## Quality bar\n\n- Motion: only `transform`, `opacity` and `clip-path` animate. UI transitions stay under 300ms with `cubic-bezier(0.23, 1, 0.32, 1)`. Hover effects only apply on `(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)`. Buttons press to `scale(0.97)`.\n- Accessibility: slides have `aria-roledescription=\"slide\"` and labels. Inactive slides are `inert` in keynote mode. The counter is `aria-live`. Focus outlines are ultramarine.\n- No horizontal overflow at 375px. No layout shift from the deck on first paint.\n";

export const KEYTU_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"KEYTU\" (showcase landing page)\n\nPaste everything below the line into your AI coding agent.\n\n---\n\nBuild a landing page in **Next.js (App Router) + React + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4** for a fictional technical mountaineering apparel brand called **KEYTU** (tagline \"Alpine equipment · Tested on K2\").\n\nThe page is a **showcase told as an ascent of K2**. A pinned mountain silhouette and an altitude meter climb with the visitor from base camp to the summit, and each camp is a product chapter. It must feel serious, precise and hand-crafted, never like a template store.\n\n**Reference** (look and feel only; generate your own images): the live template at https://openlandingpage.vercel.app/keytu, and images such as https://openlandingpage.vercel.app/templates/keytu/hero-k2-1600.webp.\n\n## Brand rules\n\n- **Black, dark blue and white only:**\n  - black `#0B0C0E`\n  - dark blue `#0F1E33`\n  - white `#F5F6F7`\n  - plus navy tints for lines and muted text: `#16294A` `#2A3D5E` `#56657F` `#A5AEBD` `#DDE1E7`\n\n  **No accent colour.** Emphasis comes only from inversion (white on navy, or black on white). Square corners, 1px hairlines, no glassy blur.\n- **All photos are tritone-mapped:** per pixel, Rec.709 luma goes to black → `#0F1E33` → white. Do this at build or processing time, not with CSS filters.\n- **Type (via `next/font/google`):**\n  - **Barlow Condensed** 800 uppercase for display and chapter titles (each title ends in a full stop), 600 for product names.\n  - **Barlow** for body text.\n  - **IBM Plex Mono** 12px uppercase with 0.1em tracking for labels, specs and altitudes.\n- **Logo:** three stacked stone ellipses plus \"KEYTU\". **Icon:** white stones on a `#0F1E33` rounded square.\n\n## Structure\n\n1. **Top bar** (fixed, 68px, solid black): logo, section links, outline \"Expedition list\" button.\n2. **Hero** (100svh): K2 photo under a black gradient. Bottom-left: mono kicker, display h1 \"Built above the treeline.\", lead paragraph, buttons \"Begin the ascent\" (solid white) and \"Expedition lookbook\" (outline).\n3. **The ascent:**\n   - **Desktop:** two columns. The left is a `position: sticky` stage (100svh under the bar). The right holds 7 chapters, each at least 100svh.\n   - **Phones:** the stage is a sticky 36svh strip under the bar, with chapters below.\n4. **Footer.**\n\n## The stage\n\n- An SVG of **K2 from Concordia** in a 1000×1000 viewBox with `xMidYMax meet`. Ridges and glacier run past the viewBox with `overflow: visible`. It shows the classic pyramid: a shadowed SW face `#16294A`, a lit E face `#2A3D5E`, a white summit cap and a pale snow shoulder, above a black glacier with faint moraine lines.\n- **The Abruzzi Spur route:** a curve from base camp (lower right) up the SE ridge to the summit. Draw it dashed in `#A5AEBD`, with the climbed part solid white via `stroke-dashoffset`. A white climber dot sits at `getPointAtLength(progress × length)` with a soft pulsing halo. Camp markers fill white when reached.\n- **Desktop altitude meter:** a vertical track whose white fill scales with `(altitude − 5000) / 3611`. Camp labels are positioned by real altitude, and the active one is white.\n- **A big readout** \"7,350 M\" with the camp name, plus coordinates `35.8825° N · 76.5133° E · Karakoram`.\n\n**Progress logic (important):**\n- Camps: Base Camp 5,000 · Camp 1 6,050 · Camp 2 6,700 · Camp 3 7,350 · Camp 4 7,900 · Bottleneck 8,200 · Summit 8,611.\n- Camp *i* is reached exactly when chapter *i*'s top reaches the arrival line. The line sits just under the top bar, plus the sticky strip on phones.\n- `progress = (i + fraction to the next chapter) / 6`. Altitude interpolates linearly between camps.\n- Set `scroll-padding-top` to the same line, so `#c3` lands on exactly \"7,350 M\".\n- Recompute on mount (covers reload mid-page), on scroll and on resize, and refresh after fonts load.\n- Use GSAP ScrollTrigger for updates, and Lenis smooth scroll synced to it. Turn Lenis off under reduced motion.\n\n## Chapters (id → content)\n\n- `base` **5,000 m:** \"The FW26 collection.\" Intro, a base-camp night photo, and a 6-product grid (cards with 1px gap lines, 4:5 image, category, name, weight · colours).\n- `c1` **6,050 m:** \"Start with what touches skin.\" Merino 200 Half-Zip feature panel with a spec list.\n- `c2` **6,700 m:** \"Three layers. One job each.\" A diagram of three bars (01 Base · 02 Mid · 03 Shell) that translate apart as the chapter scrolls. The active bar inverts to white. Plus the Grid Fleece Hoody.\n- `c3` **7,350 m:** \"The outer wall.\" Summit Shell 3L:\n  - **Colourway swap** (black, navy, white) using native radio swatches inside a `role=\"radiogroup\"`, so arrow keys work. The image crossfades in 200ms with a 2px blur during the overlap.\n  - A **gear-label spec sheet**: a white card with a dashed inner stitch outline, code `FW26-SH-01`, and mono rows Weight 412 g / Waterproof 28,000 mm / Breathability 25,000 g/m² / Fabric 3-layer, 40D face.\n  - A **fabric zoom**: a 180px 2.5× loupe for mouse users, tap-to-zoom on touch.\n- `c4` **7,900 m:** \"Nights on the Shoulder.\" 8000 Down Suit (850 FP, −40 °C, 1,980 g) with a spec sheet and fabric zoom.\n- `bottleneck` **8,200 m:** \"Through the Bottleneck.\" Summit Mitt and Alpine Gaiter spec sheets side by side.\n- `summit` **8,611 m:** \"8,611 metres. Nothing above but sky.\"\n  - A **horizontal lookbook**: scroll-snap, mouse drag, prev/next buttons, 3:2 frames. 44px hotspot buttons labelled \"Show {product}\" open a small white card with the name, weight and colours.\n  - An **expedition-list signup**: email plus \"Join the expedition list\". Error \"Enter a valid email\". On success, \"You're on the list.\" **Never send a network request.**\n\n## Images (generate, then tritone)\n\nCool, high-contrast expedition photography in the Karakoram: night and blue hour, clean silhouettes, no logos or text. Keys:\n\n- `hero-k2`, `basecamp`\n- `product-shell-black|navy|white`\n- `product-down`, `product-base`, `product-mid`, `product-hands`\n- `fabric-shell`, `fabric-down`\n- `lookbook-1..4`\n\nUntil an image exists, render a hatched navy placeholder labelled with its key.\n\n## Quality bar\n\n- Only transform, opacity and SVG attributes animate. UI transitions stay under 300ms with `cubic-bezier(0.23, 1, 0.32, 1)`. Hover only on fine pointers. Presses scale to 0.97.\n- With `prefers-reduced-motion`: no smooth scroll, no halo or beacon pulses, the layer diagram fully spread, instant swaps.\n- No horizontal page overflow at 375px (the lookbook scrolls inside its own track). Keyboard focus is a 2px white outline.\n";

