export const SATOYAMA_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"Satoyama Biyori\" — a slower kind of day\n\nBuild an original illustrated rural-Japan presentation in Next.js 16 App Router, React 19 and TypeScript. Brand: 里山日和 / Satoyama Biyori. It is a six-page picture book, with persistent chapter navigation, page dots, a counter, and previous/next arrows. It is a cultural introduction, not a tourism booking site.\n\nThe central idea: a quiet day is a picture book, so navigation turns pages through rural landscapes, daily rituals, small adventures and the seasons.\n\nUse three pastel families: cream #F6F3E8, sage #ADC3A1 and dusty rose #D7AAA0, plus dark forest ink #2F4438 for readable text. Three scene-only accents serve the painted skies and autumn: pastel sky blue #9CC4D6, warm sun #F1D9A2 and ember #C8714F. Derive every other landscape and UI tone with color-mix. Fonts through next/font/google: Shippori Mincho 400 for display and all Japanese text, DM Sans for English navigation/body. Disable CJK preloading. Use real scoped CSS custom properties for fonts and motion tokens, so webpack and Turbopack render identically without a Tailwind processing dependency.\n\nThe six chapters: Welcome; The landscape; A slower rhythm; Little adventures; The seasons; Until next time. Hero title: “A little closer to nature. A little closer to yourself.” Use oversized literary typography over an open sky, a dark leaf-motif action button, a vertical Japanese poem, and an immersive summer meadow below. Surround the scene with a cream folio frame. Header has an original mountain mark and Japanese lockup, all six chapter links and “Take the slow way.” Bottom has bilingual page labels, six dots, and arrow controls.\n\nAll artwork MUST be fully coded original SVG, never generated imagery, stock imagery or video. Paint it as 2D anime backgrounds in the spirit of Hayao Miyazaki's hand-painted scenery: gradient skies, towering cumulus with lavender undersides, layered hazy ridges, clumped leafy canopies lit from the upper left, wind-leaning grass and warm lit windows. Every slide has its own scenery, never a reused picture: Welcome is a summer meadow with a great tree on a hill; The landscape is terraced paddies mirroring the sky under misty cedar ridges; A slower rhythm is an afternoon village beside a stone canal with laundry out; Little adventures is a pale bamboo grove with sunbeams; Until next time is a dusk with the sun sinking behind ridges, paddies holding gold light, paper lanterns and fireflies. The seasons are four separate compositions, not recolours: cherry blossom over flooded paddies, summer rows of rice beneath great clouds and sunflowers, autumn paddies with rice drying on racks and a red maple, and a snowbound village with a glowing window. Share primitives (cumulus, ridge, painted tree, farmhouse, blades of grass) between scenes. Animate restrained groups: drifting clouds, mist, chimney smoke, swaying grass and bamboo, birds, falling petals, leaves or snow, fireflies. Provide a pause button, stop animation in hidden tabs and inactive pages, and stop all animation under reduced motion. Use soft gradients rather than outlines and no external assets. Do not copy existing film scenes or characters.\n\nThe landscape chapter explains satoyama as the meeting place of village and mountain. Daily rhythm is a three-entry illustrated journal, with morning, afternoon and evening. Adventures has five illustrated activity postcards: rice fields, tea, cycling, gardening and river walks. Build a looping carousel with three cards on desktop, one on mobile, next/previous controls, a live counter, swipe gestures and keyboard support. Seasons has four accessible tabs that crossfade between four different seasonal scenes and change the caption. The final chapter has a rose dusk, reflective copy and a restart button.\n\nKeep navigation logic pure and tested: clamp book edges, resolve valid hashes and wrap activity indices. Update hash on navigation; support browser back and forward and direct links. Arrow keys/Home/End navigate the book outside buttons, links and inputs. Preserve native Space and Enter. Inactive hydrated pages are inert. Without JavaScript show the complete book in normal flow. Use visible focus rings, meaningful SVG descriptions, no horizontal overflow at 375px and large touch controls.\n\nMotion verbs: drift and turn. Use a layered directional page transition, masked heading entrances, short body rises, picture hover lift and button fill wipes. Tokens: 90/180/420/800/1200ms, ease-out cubic-bezier(.23,1,.32,1), turn cubic-bezier(.77,0,.175,1). Animate only transform and opacity continuously. Organise into brand, illustrations, chapters, deck and motion folders. Keep template imports isolated. Register the route at all six gallery points, capture a real browser preview, add e2e coverage, and run repository checks, unit tests and TypeScript. Do not deploy or commit.\n";

export const PIPKIN_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"Pipkin\" (a pastel jam-shop landing page that follows one jar from order to doorstep)\n\nPaste everything below the line into your AI coding agent.\n\n---\n\nBuild a complete, isolated landing template in **Next.js 16 (App Router) + React 19 + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4** for a fictional two-person jam shop called **Pipkin**. It is the kind of small shop that runs on an e-commerce platform: a shelf, a box builder, shipping you can follow. Install one extra package: `npm i lenis`. There are **no photographs**: every picture is hand-drawn SVG in the page's palette.\n\nThe page's one idea: **every small shop has a trip between \"order placed\" and \"on the doorstep\", so the page follows one jar along that trip.** It must feel alive and premium on every interaction (load, scroll, hover, press, focus, forms), never like a component kit.\n\n## Brand\n\n- **Voice:** warm, specific, a little cheeky. Real numbers: 36 jars a batch, 2 cooks, 72 hours stove to door, 31 countries, $9 a jar, free shipping from 6 jars. Tagline: \"Cooked Tuesday. At your door Friday.\"\n- **Palette: eight colours, nothing else.** Pastels with one dark ink. Make tints with `color-mix()` of these only; no gradients, no extra hues, no textures, no halftone, no brutalist or utilitarian styling.\n  - cream `#FFF8EC` (paper, jar glass, inputs)\n  - plum `#3B2A47` (ink: all text and every outline)\n  - blush `#FFD6E2` (tint, shelf ground)\n  - bubblegum `#FF9DBB` (the single action colour: primary buttons, the headline highlight pill)\n  - butter `#FFEBA6` (lids, box, batch ground)\n  - mint `#CBEBDA` (leaf, success, list ground)\n  - sky `#CDE5F8` (order scene, box builder ground)\n  - lilac `#E3D8F6` (plum jam, notes ground)\n- **Type (all `next/font/google`, distinct CSS variable names):** **Bagel Fat One** 400 for display and prices; **Nunito** (variable, weights 600–800) for body at 17px/1.6; **Sometype Mono** 11–13px uppercase with 0.1em tracking for eyebrows, step lines, tags and SVG labels. Sizes with `clamp()`: h1 2.9–6.4rem, h2 2.1–4rem.\n- **Shape:** everything is a pill or heavily rounded (999px for controls, 28–56px for cards and slabs). Every shape has a 2.5–3px plum outline.\n- **Logo:** a squat jar mark (bubblegum body, butter lid, cream label, plum line) beside the word \"Pipkin\" in Bagel Fat One. Provide `icon.svg` and a 1200×630 `opengraph-image.tsx` (palette only).\n- **Headlines** are split into word masks; one word or phrase per heading sits on a bubblegum highlight pill (an inline-block with a `::before` that wipes in).\n\n## Structure (a folder, not one file)\n\nEverything lives in `src/app/(pipkin)/` and imports only npm packages and its own files (no `@/` aliases). `layout.tsx` (fonts, metadata, `<noscript>` override), `pipkin/page.tsx` (server component inside an `OrderProvider`), `pipkin.css` + `trip.css` + `shop.css`, `brand/` (Logo, Jar, Marks), `data/shop.ts`, `box/` (logic, context), `trip/` (progress, TripStage, Traveler, scenes), `sections/`, `forms/`, `motion/`. Client components are leaves only. Target 40 files, about 3,000 lines.\n\n## The sections\n\n1. **Header:** fixed pill bar; compresses with a blurred background after 80px, hides on scroll-down and returns on scroll-up; a nav indicator that slides between links and follows the active section; a cart pill showing the jar count; a Magnetic \"Order\" button; below 960px a round toggle that morphs to ✕ and opens a full-screen butter menu with a circle clip-path wipe, staggered links, scroll lock, focus trap and Esc to close.\n2. **Hero:** grid with copy left and art right. An \"Order #0412 placed · Mon 9:41\" pill, the h1 \"Cooked [[Tuesday.]] At your door Friday.\", a lede, two CTAs (\"Build a box\", \"Follow a jar\"), three facts. Art: a sky circle with three jars (lilac \"No. 05\", bubblegum \"No. 01\", butter \"No. 02\"), three chips (\"Made Tuesday\", \"Real fruit, no pectin\", \"Batch No. 17\") and sparkles. Four soft pastel blobs drift behind.\n3. **Flavour band:** a rotated bubblegum strip with a looping marquee of six flavour pills.\n4. **The Trip (the signature mechanic):** below.\n5. **The Shelf:** six flavour cards (Strawberry & Basil, Meyer Lemon Marmalade, Pear & Vanilla Bean, Blueberry & Thyme, Plum & Lavender, Peach & Chamomile) with filter chips (All six / Fruity / Floral / Tangy), $9 each, an \"Add to box\" button.\n6. **Build a Box:** size toggle (3 / 6 / 12 jars), a yellow box of slots that fill with jars, a stepper per flavour, a live total (1 jar $9; $1 off from 3, $4 off from 6, $12 off at 12; shipping $6 under 6 jars, free from 6), \"Place order\", loading, then a success ticket. The shelf buttons and the header counter share the same box state.\n7. **Batch Day:** four count-up stats (36 jars, 2 cooks, 72 h, 1,208 notes) and a Mon–Fri week strip joined by a drawn dashed line.\n8. **Doorstep Notes:** two rows of customer-note cards drifting in opposite directions, pausing on hover.\n9. **Questions:** five-item accordion (height via `grid-template-rows`), one open at a time.\n10. **The Friday List:** a signup form (first name, email, a \"which jar do you lean towards?\" pill radio group). No network: validate on the client, shake invalid fields, show a loading button, then a circle wipe to a success card.\n11. **Footer:** plum slab, link columns, a giant \"pipkin\" wordmark that rises through a mask.\n\nSections are rounded slabs (56px top radius) that overlap the one above by 56px, each on its own pastel ground (cream, blush, sky, butter, lilac, cream, mint, then plum). Content scales to 0.96 and fades as the next slab slides over (`animation-timeline: view()` under `@supports`).\n\n## The mechanic: The Trip\n\nA tall section (`520vh`) with a `position: sticky` full-height pin. Inside, a rounded stage shows **five illustrated SVG scenes side by side** (a 500%-wide strip translated by `calc(var(--s) * -20%)`). **One jar rides the route** through them:\n\n1. **Order placed:** a phone on the counter with a notification, a ringing bell, a receipt ticket; the jar is a dashed ghost outline. Sky.\n2. **Cooking:** a lilac stockpot of bubbling jam, a stirring spoon, steam, a ladle pouring a stream; the jar fills with jam as you scroll. Butter.\n3. **Packed:** tape, tissue, stickers, a pencil and a hanging \"Handle with jam\" tag; the jar gets its lid and sits in an open box, the box lid beside it. Mint.\n4. **Out for delivery:** a pink pickup with a flat bed carrying the closed box, a scrolling road, houses and trees; wheels spin with scroll. Lilac.\n5. **Delivered:** a front door, windows, a mat and a plant; the box lid lifts off and lands beside it, the jar pops up and settles, sparkles twinkle, a \"Delivered · 11:20\" banner appears. Blush.\n\nLogic (pure functions with tests): `sectionProgress(scrollY, top, height, vh)` → 0..1; `dwell(p, stops)` eases each segment with a double smoothstep so the page lingers on each stop; `nearestStop`. **One rAF-throttled scroll listener** writes `--s` (0..4) and `--p` (0..1) on the stage; React state changes only when the stop changes. Each scene gets `--d = s - i` and focus `--f`; three parallax layers shift by `d * -90px`, `d * -36px`, `d * 64px`; jam level is `clamp(0, (s - 0.7) / 0.6, 1)`. The jar/box is an SVG overlay with the same viewBox and crop as the scenes, with a CSS state machine on `data-stop` and spring transitions; a squash-and-settle replays on every stop by alternating two identical keyframe names by stop parity. Add a tracker pill (\"Your jar · Cooking\"), a route bar with a riding mini jar, a rail of five numbered buttons (jump via a custom event handled by the smooth scroller), an `aria-live` caption, and `#trip-3` deep links.\n\n**Fallback:** with `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` or no JavaScript the trip renders as five stacked cards, each with its own scene, a static jar and its caption, all visible.\n\n## Motion (organic / analog, expressive)\n\nVerbs: **settle** and **wobble**, with **unfurl** for lids and reveals. Signature moment: the last stop (lid lifts, jar pops and settles, banner, tracker flips).\n\nTokens in `@theme static`: `--ease-out cubic-bezier(.23,1,.32,1)`, `--ease-out-expo cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1)`, `--ease-in-out cubic-bezier(.77,0,.175,1)`, `--ease-spring linear(0,.35 8%,.82 20%,1.06 30%,1.02 42%,.99 58%,1)`; durations 90/180/420/800/1200ms; reveal shift 28px; stagger 70ms. No raw cubic-beziers or ms values in components.\n\n- **Load choreography (CSS keyframes, before hydration):** background settles at 0ms, logo and nav drop at 150ms (items staggered 50ms), headline words rise through masks from 300ms, highlight wipes at ~950ms, lede, CTAs and facts follow, jars drop and settle on springs from 650ms, chips and sparkles pop, ambient loops start after ~1.4s.\n- **Reveal vocabulary (at least six, opacity is never alone):** mask-rise words, highlight wipe, slide-in eyebrows, rise, scale-settle, springy pop with a tiny rotation, line draw, clip-path unfurl (ticket), circle wipe (list success, menu), count-up with the final value in a visually hidden span.\n- **Scroll:** Lenis smooth scroll (fine pointers only, off under reduced motion), a page progress hairline, the pinned trip, section slab handoffs, one hero parallax via `animation-timeline: view()`.\n- **Every interactive element has rest, hover (fine pointers only), focus-visible, pressed (`scale(.96)`, released on a spring), disabled, and loading/success where relevant.** Buttons wipe a fill from the left and nudge their arrow; primary CTAs are Magnetic; cards tilt up to ±6° with a pointer-following glow and dim their neighbours; steppers shrink on press; the size toggle's thumb slides on a spring; inputs have floating labels and an animated ring; invalid fields shake once.\n- **Ambient (max three loops in view, paused off-screen):** hero blobs drift, hero jars breathe, the order dot pings; in the trip only the active scene loops and only while visible; marquees pause on hover and focus.\n- **Craft rules:** animate only `transform`, `opacity` and `clip-path`; scroll and pointer values go to CSS custom properties from one rAF loop, never React state; hidden pre-reveal states exist only under `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, with a `<noscript>` override; under reduced motion everything is visible and finished-looking with no parallax, loops or smooth scroll; on touch, magnetic and tilt are off and nothing depends on hover.\n\n## Quality bar\n\nOne `h1`, logical headings, landmarks, `lang=\"en\"`, real buttons and links with names, visible focus rings, AA contrast (plum on every pastel), `aria-live` only for meaningful changes (the stop caption, the box notice). Images: none. Mobile Lighthouse targets: performance ≥ 90, accessibility ≥ 95, best practices 100; CLS 0; no horizontal overflow at 375px.\n\nAdd unit tests for the mechanic (trip progress), the motion maths, the box reducer and totals, the shop data and form validation; add Playwright tests for the trip's stops and deep link, the box builder, the Friday list, keyboard handling, reduced motion and the phone viewport.\n";

export const MIO_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild Mio Hazuki — a digital artist's little worlds\n\nBuild an isolated, complete Next.js 16 App Router + React + TypeScript + Tailwind v4 landing template for a fictional digital illustrator, Mio Hazuki, at /mio. The purpose is personal commissions. Runtime dependency: lenis, dynamically loaded on fine-pointer desktop only.\n\n## World and concept\nEvery commission begins with a small wish, so the hero's paper lantern lifts from the artist's seaside studio as the visitor scrolls toward the commission invitation. Use original Japanese animation inspired 2D scenery, everyday magic, quiet intimacy and nostalgic atmosphere. No 3D, model files, stock photography, generated hero images, copied characters, or existing studio branding. All images, including portfolio samples, are original inline SVG. The user explicitly requested coded scenery and waived brand-board approval.\n\n## Identity\nMotto: Little worlds, made just for you. Voice: sincere, observant, gentle, specific. Fonts through next/font/google: Kaisei Decol 400/500 display (preload false), Karla body, Inconsolata for price/year data. Distinct variable names. Eight colors only: ink #232C49, paper #F5EFE4, hydrangea #A8B1D1, sea #526D89, apricot #EDAD83, coral #BC6268, plum #654C65, cloud #D8CAD9. Use color-mix and SVG gradients for tints. Readable ink-on-paper and paper-on-ink text. Original lantern-and-wave SVG logo, favicon and next/og share card.\n\n## Composition\nThe full-bleed, viewport-height hero is a rich twilight harbor with a small tiled-roof studio on the right-hand cliff, illuminated windows, layered mountains, distant waterfront houses, a sailboat, grasses and little flowers. A cloudbank and moon fill the sky. Left-set serif headline: A little wonder, made just for you. Intro: Hi, I'm Mio. I draw quiet adventures, curious souls, and places that feel a little like home. Primary CTA goes to commissions; secondary goes to work. Slim navigation, availability note, pause control and shoreline page transition. Mobile explicitly repositions the SVG below the headline so the studio remains visible.\n\nPortfolio: four independent SVG compositions and a staggered editorial grid, filter buttons, and native detail dialogs with Escape and focus restoration. Works: Where the light stays (coastal home), The cloud keeper (original traveler with a cloud jar and a white fox), One last delivery (bicycle outside a lit night shop), An afternoon, forever (cat beside an open-window artist's desk). Label these as original sample works for a fictional portfolio.\n\nAbout: illustrated artist at her desk, a personal note about everyday wonder, and a signature. Pricing: A familiar face, character portrait from $120, 1–2 weeks; A little moment, character and setting from $280, 2–3 weeks; A world of your own, full scene from $560, 3–5 weeks. Clearly list scope, revisions, digital delivery, personal use, commercial quote, and 50% deposit/balance. These are sample prices, not real claims. Selecting a scope preselects it in the form.\n\nFour-stage process: daydream, agreed quote and sketch, revisions and color, final delivery. Dark invitation: Your daydream. My paintbrush. Form: name, email, package, idea. Validate name/email, allowlisted package and story length. Show errors with focus on the first invalid input, then loading and a lantern success state. It is a clearly labeled local demo: no network request or storage, no assertion of a sent message. Let the visitor copy/read the prepared brief and start another. Finish with four native FAQ disclosures and a footer: logo, tagline and availability note, Explore and Commissions link columns that jump to real sections, a bottom row with copyright, back to the harbor and a start-a-brief link, then a full-width original SVG scene that fades up out of the paper — a lantern-lit seaside village on a headland, a red-and-white lighthouse, a sailboat, round blossoming trees and grass with coral flowers, with a paper lantern floating above the village. It uses only the eight palette colors, drifts clouds, bobs the lantern and sways the foreground in three ambient groups that pause offscreen and under reduced motion.\n\n## Motion and quality\nOrganic / analog, verbs sway and unfurl. Masked hero lines, staggered nav/copy/actions, three restrained ambient groups, small 2D layer offsets, one scroll-driven lantern ascent. Lantern progress is clamp(scrollY / heroHeight,0,1), x=-45p, y=-170p, rotation=-4+9p. A page progress hairline. Use shared cubic-bezier(.23,1,.32,1), cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1), cubic-bezier(.77,0,.175,1), spring and 90/180/420/800/1200ms tokens. Reveal vocabulary: rise, mask, wipe, scale and draw. Hover lifts artwork, scales media and translates arrows; press feedback scale .97. No CSS 3D, tilt, autoplay audio or hover-only information. Stop ambient work offscreen and in hidden tabs; provide a scene pause button. Reduced motion disables position movement and Lenis. Without JS all content and art stay visible. Real semantic controls, strong contrast, visible focus, usable 390px touch layout and no horizontal overflow.\n\nSplit into brand, art, harbor, sections, works, forms and motion folders. Test pure progress/lantern math and validation, plus browser navigation, filters, dialog, scope preselection, local success, mobile menu, reduced motion and no-JS. Register palette, gallery prompt and screenshot preview, copy bundle, image-processing allowlist, metadata expectations and shared e2e path. Include DESIGN.md and optional replacement-art directions. All delivered art is complete; no media waiting on the user. Do not deploy or commit.\n";

