export const FLORI_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"Flori\" — a little flower club\n\nBuild an isolated Next.js 16 App Router + React 19 + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4 landing page at `/flori` for a fictional seasonal flower subscription. Install `npm i next react react-dom lenis`. Read the installed Next documentation before implementing APIs. Keep the complete copy unit inside `src/app/(flori)/`.\n\nThe idea: flowers gather stem by stem, so the page lets you compose a bouquet that follows you into signup. Satsuma is a quality reference for playful depth and craftsmanship. Invent the botanical art, geometry and identity from scratch. The mood is airy, playful, tender, sculptural, sunlit. No brutalism, utilitarian styling, halftones, grain, dotted textures, stock imagery, gradients, heavy borders or black.\n\nPalette: cream #FFF9F0 page, fern #344C40 text and actions, blush #F1C6CF, lilac #D8D1EC, pistachio #D9E6C8, butter #F5DF9B, apricot #EDB997, sage #91AAA0. Derive tints with color-mix. Use fern text on pastel backgrounds. Fonts through next/font/google: Lora 500 normal/italic for expressive display, Outfit 400/500/600 for body and labels, Caveat 500 for handwritten notes. Distinct font variables. A six-petal flower beside a lowercase literary `flori` wordmark is the logo; include the SVG favicon and a branded 1200×630 OG image.\n\nHero: quiet sticky navigation; centered eyebrow; two-line headline \"A little bloom. / A brighter day.\" with the second line italic; two lines of supporting copy; five fanned botanical postcards on pastel backgrounds with different vase silhouettes and original vector cosmos, poppies, daisies and bells. Use rotation ±14 degrees and a shallow arc; center card highest. Handwritten marginal note and a green circular flower seal flank the hero on desktop. A dark fern pill \"Find your flowers ↗\" sits below the fan. Include the price line \"From £24 a bunch · A little joy, on repeat\". Keep the complete hero visible on a 1440×900 laptop. On phones crop the fan gracefully to about three postcards without horizontal page overflow.\n\nSections: scalloped pistachio promise band; weekly gathering with three large illustrated cards (Daydream, Sunbeam, Wild Thing); lilac bouquet composer; asymmetric illustrated club story with heading \"Not for a birthday. Not for an apology. Just for living.\"; pistachio subscription plans; local-only signup; blush oversized typographic footer.\n\nMake the hero postcards a moving carousel: drift continuously at 24px/s and recompute each card's arc height and rotation from its horizontal position. Wrap ten slots outside the viewport, with the second set reusing the original botanical SVG groups via `<use>`, aria-hidden and excluded from keyboard tab order. Pause on hover, focus, hidden tabs, offscreen, and local/global pause controls. Add previous/next buttons, keyboard arrows on the carousel region, and horizontal pointer dragging with restrained release momentum. Focused original cards move into view immediately. With reduced motion, stop autoplay and momentum while keeping manual controls instant. Without JavaScript, retain the original five-card fan. Put tested wrapping and arc geometry in `bouquet/carousel.ts`, with the client controller in `bouquet/PostcardCarousel.tsx`; keep per-frame values out of React state.\n\nComposer: native radio controls for Daydream (pink/lilac), Sunbeam (apricot/butter), Wild Thing (cream/lilac). Sizes little £24, lovely £34, abundant £48. Seven original SVG stem groups gather from their scattered positions as the section enters the viewport; changing the feeling updates the petals and vase. A measured, clamped scroll progress is calculated by a tested pure function. Only CSS custom properties are written in the requestAnimationFrame scroll loop. The CTA preserves feeling and size in `?feeling=sunbeam&size=lovely#join`; collection cards deep-link to the composer. Parse and allowlist query values; defaults are daydream/little. Intercept enhanced hash anchors to scroll to the real section, preserving keyboard, history and modified clicks.\n\nSignup: first name and email, prefilled flower feeling and size. Validate names up to 80 characters, email up to 254 characters, allowlisted choices. Show associated error text, aria-invalid, focus the first invalid field, and animate one gentle shake. Submit visibly prepares then replaces the form with a flower confirmation containing the name and selected bunch. No network request, storage, order placement, or payment. Make its demo status clear. Reset returns to the form and focuses the name field. No-JS shows complete art and an email contact fallback.\n\nMotion identity: gather and unfurl. Build motion tokens first: ease-out cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1), expo-out cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1), in-out cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1), spring linear(0,.5 15%,1.03 50%,1); duration 90/180/420/850/1200ms; stagger 75ms; shift 26px. Choreograph header, masked headline lines, lead, postcard fan, CTA and notes on load. At least four reveals: masks, card cascades, media wipes and drawn rules. Buttons fill-wipe, arrows slide, cards lift, native radios show visible focus. Responsive native dialog navigation with Escape, focus containment and return, and scroll lock. Compact header and progress rail. Dynamically import Lenis only for fine pointers without reduced motion; use native touch scrolling. Ambient movement is limited to leaf sway and a flower turn, paused offscreen, in hidden tabs, and by the footer pause control. No spatial motion, hidden content, ambient loops, or Lenis under reduced motion. No-JS and reduced-motion versions look finished.\n\nUse server page and section components with client leaves for interactions. Split into brand, content, bouquet, motion, forms and sections folders. Include DESIGN.md with a full motion map, this rebuild prompt, media directions and an optional responsive Picture helper. All production artwork is code-drawn SVG and complete without external media. Test the scroll math, query handling, validation, bouquet controls, form flow, keyboard navigation, mobile menu, no-JS, reduced-motion and 390px overflow. Register palette, image-processing allowlist, copy metadata, gallery entry with actual screenshot, gallery tests and shared e2e paths. Run repository guards, all unit tests, TypeScript, Playwright and mobile Lighthouse. Do not deploy or commit.\n";
export const CULLET_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"Cullet\" (a real-time material studio whose hero is a slab of cut glass)\n\nPaste everything below the line into your AI coding agent.\n\n---\n\nBuild a landing page in **Next.js 16 (App Router) + React 19 + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4** at `/cullet` for a fictional company called **Cullet** (Cullet Lab): a browser studio for authoring real-time glass materials (dispersion, transmission, refraction, roughness, a thin film) and exporting them as plain glTF with the KHR material extensions, USDZ, or a typed three.js module. Slogan: *\"Designed in glass.\"* Plans: Sketch $0, Studio $24 a seat ($19 billed yearly), Foundry custom. Install `npm i next react react-dom three lenis`. Read the installed Next docs before using any API. Keep the whole copy unit inside `src/app/(cullet)/`, importing only npm packages and files from that folder.\n\n**The central idea: glass is cut, not drawn, so the page is a slab of frosted quartz cleaved along its cut lines.** The hero is a slab of eleven extruded glass shards carrying the page's text; scrolling cleaves it apart to reveal the next chapter; \"Read paper\" shatters it into a technical note and \"Back\" reassembles it; further down, one piece of glass becomes a live sampler.\n\nMood: clinical, high-key, optical, quiet, precise. Number the chapters like a lab notebook (Chapter 01 … 07). Specific numbers only: 1.6 ms per material at 1080p, 60 fps with dispersion on a three-year-old phone, 0 baked maps, 41,200 materials cut.\n\n## Brand\n\n- **Palette (eight colours; tints only via `color-mix()`, registered in `scripts/templates.config.ts`):** ink `#0F1117` (text, the CTA pill, the Open slab), paper `#F4F5F8` (page), white `#FFFFFF` (the glass, cards), slate `#525866` (muted text), mist `#9AA1B2` (hairlines; muted text on ink only), haze `#DCE8FF` (cool fill light, tints), cobalt `#2563EB` (the one accent: links, focus, selection, chapter labels), emerald `#10B981` (rare: CTA hover, success, live dots). One palette-exempt file, `sampler/spectrum.ts`, holds six wavelength stops (650–420 nm) used only where a ray has split.\n- **Type (`next/font/google`, distinct variables):** Inter 400/500/600 (`--font-fc-inter`) for body and the hero print; Inter Tight 500/600 (`--font-fc-tight`) for display, tracking −0.03 to −0.05em; Geist Mono 400/500 (`--font-fc-mono`, `preload: false`) for chapter labels, codes and readouts.\n- **Logo:** an ink tile (rx 5.4 in a 24 viewBox) with one cut shard traced in paper, `M5.3 6.7 13.4 4.3 19.7 12.5 10.6 19.7Z`, beside \"Cullet\" in Inter Tight 600. Ship `brand/Logo.tsx`, `icon.svg` (with one cobalt edge) and a 1200×630 `opengraph-image.tsx` drawing the cut slab behind \"Designed / in glass.\".\n- **Corners:** rounded. Shards 20·s, cards 20–24px, buttons 8–10px, the hero CTA 6px.\n- **Signature details:** text printed on the glass and cut by the gaps (yet still selectable); a numbered chapter rail with a sliding glass pill; live readouts (frame time, Abbe number, the glTF line that flashes as you turn a dial); catalogue codes CU-152…; the slab's l1→J1 cut line recurring as the materials sheet's top edge and as a cobalt cut through the footer wordmark; `KHR_materials_dispersion` as a recurring caption.\n\n## The hero: a cut slab of quartz (port this exactly)\n\nPure, tested geometry in `cut/`:\n\n- **The cut** in region units (x right, y down). Edge points `t1 (.27,0) t2 (.68,0) r1 (1,.48) b1 (.44,1) l1 (0,.36) l2 (0,.78)` and junctions `J1 (.34,.28) J2 (.62,.24) J3 (.81,.41) J4 (.26,.57) J5 (.51,.61) J6 (.72,.69)`, each with periods `px, py` (11.9–18.2 s) and a phase. Each point drifts `sin(2πt/px + φ)·0.017` in x and `cos(2πt/py + 1.3φ)·0.017` in y (edge points move only along their edge), and moves away from the pointer within 0.48 by `(1 − d/0.48)²·0.028`. Eight shards: `[TL,t1,J1,l1] [t1,t2,J2,J1] [t2,TR,r1,J3,J2] [l1,J1,J4,l2] [J1,J2,J5,J4] [J2,J3,J6,J5] [l2,J4,J5,b1,BL] [b1,J5,J6,J3,r1,BR]`, which tile the panel at any time. The nav is three shards split by two slanted cuts (landscape 0.25→0.29 and 0.79→0.75 across its height; portrait 0.30→0.33 and 0.78→0.74), swaying ±0.007.\n- **Glass metrics:** `s = slabW / 1730`; inset every polygon by half of a `14·s` gap (mitre offset); round corners with quadratic tangents of radius `20·s`, capped at 42% of an edge; extrude `14·s` with a `3·s` bevel.\n- **Layout:** the slab is inset `8.5svh 5.5vw` (16px on phones) inside a sticky 100svh stage of a 230svh section. Nav band `max(48px, 10.8% of slab height)`, gap `max(8px, 14·s)`, the panel below. Panel text sized with container units: eyebrow \"REAL-TIME MATERIAL STUDIO\" (tracked 0.2em), headline \"Designed / in glass.\" `clamp(38px, min(7.8cqw, 20cqh), 220px)` weight 600, −0.045em, line-height 0.97; sub copy \"Author dispersion, transmission, and refraction in the browser. / Live previews, real materials.\"; an ink \"Open studio\" pill and \"Read paper →\"; a hairline registration ring; footer \"© Cullet Lab\" and \"KHR_materials_dispersion\". Nav: mark + \"Cullet\" | \"Studio · Materials · Pricing\" | \"Sign in\".\n- **WebGL (three.js `WebGLRenderer`, `MeshPhysicalMaterial`):** white glass with emissive `0xf0f4fa` at 0.35, roughness 0.22, clearcoat 1 at 0.08, IOR 1.52, iridescence 0.65 (IOR 1.35, 280–680 nm), env 1.15, under a `RoomEnvironment` PMREM (0.85) and a high-key rig (ambient 1.35, hemisphere white/haze 1.15, key 2.2 from upper left, cool fill 1.35 from below right, rim 1.1). A haze-coloured shadow plane sits behind each shard. Camera FOV 30 placed so 1 world unit = 1 CSS px. The page group tilts toward the pointer (rotateY `x·3.5° − 1.2°`, rotateX `y·2.75° + 0.8°`, τ 0.3 s). The shard under the pointer lifts 50·s (nav 32·s) and tips up to ±0.24 rad on a spring (k 170, c 19), and its iridescence rises to 1 and env to 2. `onBeforeCompile` adds a cobalt and rose blush that follows the pointer (`exp(−1.6·(d/max(120, 300s))²)`). Rebuild the drifting geometry at ~45 fps.\n- **Printed text, real DOM:** the DOM is the layout and a canvas is the ink. `print.ts` draws every `[data-print]` element (`text` / `box` / `mark` / `ring`) at its offset in its own computed font, letter-spacing and `--print` / `--print-bg` / `--print-line` variables (hover and focus restyle those, and the print redraws once per frame). Each shard's cap uses UVs mapped to the whole region. The DOM stays on top, transparent, mapped onto the tilted glass with a homography: solve the 8-DOF projective map from the plane's rectangle to its four projected corners and apply it as `matrix3d()` with `transform-origin: 0 0`. Text stays selectable and every link focusable.\n- **Poster first:** draw the same cut as eleven HTML-positioned SVGs (frosted gradient, white rim, two drop-shadows), server-rendered for a laptop and a phone guess and then refit to the real box. WebGL takes over on idle with a fine pointer; on touch screens only after a \"Light the glass\" tap; never under reduced motion. The handover is a short lift-and-land, not a fly-in.\n- **Cleave (scroll):** `cleave = range(pinProgress, 0.04, 0.82)`; per shard `easeInCubic(range(c, 0.035i, 0.035i + 0.62))`; each flies `520·s·f` along its outward direction (from the slab centre) and `(460–800)·s·f` toward the camera, tumbling; the poster does the same in CSS from `--cleave`. Behind the glass, \"Chapter 01 / Real-time glass, in five terms.\" fades up. The slab ignores pointers while cleaving.\n- **Shatter (Read paper):** ease-in-cubic over 0.72 s, 30 ms stagger, with gravity `640·s·f²`; at 280 ms a dialog opens: \"Technical note 07 · Dispersion in a single forward pass.\" with three paragraphs, three figures and a link to the terms, beside a skewed quartz sliver behind \"← Back\" that wobbles and tips toward the pointer. Back or Escape reassembles (back-ease, 0.12 s wait) and returns focus to Read paper. The slab is `inert` and the scroll locked while it is open. Without JS, Read paper is a link to `#terms`.\n\n## Page\n\n1. **Header** (fixed glass bar): lockup, Terms · Studio · Materials · Pricing with a cobalt indicator that slides (transform only) to the current section, \"Open studio\". It arrives once the slab has cleaved, tucks away on scroll-down and returns on scroll-up, and has a page-progress hairline. On phones: a menu sheet that opens with a clip-path circle from the toggle, staggers its links, traps focus, closes on Escape and gives focus back.\n2. **Terms** (`#terms`, Chapter 01, held for 5 × 78svh on ≥960px): a rail of five numbered terms (Iridescence, Index of refraction, Dispersion, Transmission, Roughness) with a sliding glass pill; one glass figure at a time with an SVG ray diagram (thin film, Snell, prism with a spectral fan, a frosted slab scattering, micro-shards with narrow and wide cones), a readout chip, `01 / 05`, the term and its definition. Each step wipes the figure in on a diagonal and draws its rays in order. The rail jumps to a step. Elsewhere it is five stacked cards.\n3. **Studio** (`#sampler`, Chapter 02, \"Turn the dials. Watch the light.\"): a white sheet that slides over the held terms. A 4:3 stage (WebGL near the viewport on desktop, on tap on touch) with a 10-face lathe gem in transmissive `MeshPhysicalMaterial` in front of a striped studio card, turning slowly and dragged with inertia, with a HUD (\"Live · WebGL\" and the measured frame time). Six preset chips; five range dials (IOR 1–2.4, dispersion 0–1, roughness 0–1, thickness 0.1–4 cm, iridescence 0–1); the Abbe number; and a glTF readout (`KHR_materials_ior/_dispersion/_transmission/_volume/_iridescence`) whose changed line flashes, with \"Copy JSON\". Before WebGL, an SVG poster answers every dial: the fan bends `34°·(ior−1)`, spreads `46°·d·(ior−1)`, blurs with roughness, and a film tint follows iridescence.\n4. **Workflow** (`#workflow`, Chapter 03, \"Cut. Preview. Ship.\"): three steps joined by a drawn cobalt line, each with a working surface: a layer stack, three renderer tiles with ms readouts, and a code snippet.\n5. **Materials** (`#materials`, Chapter 04, \"Six cuts to start from.\"): a white sheet whose top edge is a cut line that levels out as it enters. Six cards (Crown silica CU-152, Dense flint CU-172, Frosted quartz CU-154, Borosilicate CU-147, Sapphire CU-177, Opal film CU-145) with palette-only CSS glass swatches, IOR / Abbe / roughness, a use line, and \"Open in studio\", which loads the preset into the sampler and travels there.\n6. **Proof** (`#proof`, Chapter 05, \"Glass that fits in a frame.\"): four count-up stats with drawn rules, a marquee of renderers that read the export, three studio notes.\n7. **Pricing** (`#pricing`, Chapter 06, \"Pay for the seat, not the render.\"): a monthly/yearly radio switch with a sliding pill; three cuts; prices roll in on change.\n8. **Open** (`#open`, Chapter 07, ink): \"Your first glass is ten minutes away.\" with a seat form: work email, a role chip group, team size. Rules in `forms/request.ts` (tested). Invalid fields shake once, messages slide down, and focus goes to the first error. Submit shows \"Cutting your seat…\", then the form cleaves away and a glass shard settles with an emerald tick: \"Your seat is cut.\" with a stable seat number and a starter material. No network request.\n9. **Footer:** lockup, index, a giant wordmark with a cobalt cut through it, \"© 2026 Cullet Lab · a fictional brand for a landing-page template\", \"Back to the slab ↑\".\n\n## Motion\n\n- **Identity:** precise and physical. Verbs: **cleave** and **settle**. Signature moment: the scroll-scrubbed cleave of the hero slab.\n- **Tokens in `@theme`:** out `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`, out-expo `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`, in-out `cubic-bezier(0.77, 0, 0.175, 1)`, in `cubic-bezier(0.55, 0, 0.9, 0.35)` (the shatter only), back `cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.45, 0.64, 1)`, spring `linear(0, 0.35 8%, 0.82 20%, 1.06 30%, 1.02 42%, 0.99 58%, 1)`; durations 90 / 180 / 300 (hover fills, label rolls, toggles) / 420 / 800 / 1200 ms; shift 28px; stagger 70ms.\n- **Load (CSS keyframes, before hydration):** backdrop settles from 1.08, poster settles from an 8px blur, nav items drop at 150ms + 50ms, headline rises through masks, copy and CTAs follow.\n- **Reveals (one IntersectionObserver flips `data-in`):** mask-rise titles, slide labels, rise copy, scale-rise card cascades, diagonal clip-path cleave wipes (clip the child, observe the parent), drawn lines and rays, count-ups.\n- **States:** buttons fill-wipe and roll their label (`content: attr(data-text) / \"\"`), press to .97 with a spring release; cards lift with a pointer spotlight, a swatch sheen and dimmed siblings; links draw their underline; the header indicator, rail pill and pricing pill slide.\n- **One scroll owner** (`motion/ScrollDirector.tsx`) writes `--cleave`, the terms' `--p` and active step, the materials' `--enter`, page progress and the header state from a single rAF. Lenis (lerp 0.1) runs on fine pointers with motion allowed and answers `fc:goto` and `fc:lock`.\n- **Reduced motion and no JS:** hidden states live inside `(prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (scripting: enabled)`; neither WebGL engine mounts; nothing is held; a `<noscript>` reveal override.\n\n## Quality bar\n\n- Animate only transforms, opacity and clip-path (plus the one-shot load blur). CLS 0. No horizontal overflow at 375px.\n- One h1, landmarks, real buttons and links, AA contrast, visible focus rings (they survive on the transparent DOM over the glass), a labelled paper dialog with a focus trap.\n- Structure: `brand/`, `cut/` (geometry, pattern, homography + tests), `hero/` (poster, engine, print, controller), `terms/`, `sampler/` (params + tests, engine, poster), `sections/`, `forms/` (rules + tests), `content/` (typed data + tests), `motion/` (math, progress + tests, primitives, the director).\n- Playwright covers the WebGL takeover and the homography transform, the scroll cleave, the paper (focus, inert, Escape, Back), the held terms and rail, the dials and the glTF readout, presets and \"Open in studio\", pricing, the form (errors, focus, success, no request), reveals and hover, reduced motion (no WebGL, nothing hidden), and the phone (tap to light, menu, no overflow).\n";

export const UNDERSTUDY_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"Understudy\" (a rehearsal studio for first jobs, with a scroll that pushes you through the stage)\n\nPaste everything below the line into your AI coding agent.\n\n---\n\nBuild a landing page in **Next.js 16 (App Router) + React 19 + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4** at `/understudy` for a fictional company called **Understudy**: a rehearsal studio for first jobs. You play a real role (a *part*) at one of eighteen simulated companies, with a *cast* (team), a *director* (manager) and real briefs, and leave with a *reel* (portfolio) that the 3,400 companies *casting* from the studio can open. Opening night (the first week) is free, then $89 a month. Slogan: *\"Your first job, rehearsed.\"* Install `npm i next react react-dom lenis`. Read the installed Next docs before using any API. Keep the whole copy unit inside `src/app/(understudy)/`, importing only npm packages and files from that folder.\n\n**Reference (look, pacing and motion vocabulary; build your own brand):** learnwithzero.com: one held hero that zooms into the logo mark until its black fills the screen, a figure that rises digit by digit over two rows of logos bent through a wide lens, a row of tall cards (here an endless drifting ring), a held line a colour wave runs through, a scroll-drawn thread, a matching stage with an odometer, a signed offer letter between two words, a living world at the foot, a glass dock as the only navigation. Every name, mark, illustration, line of copy and company here is invented.\n\n**The central idea: a stage you push into, so the page scrolls you through a proscenium into the dark house and then runs like a show.** The logo mark is a proscenium arch, and the same arch is the dark house's `clip-path` in the hero.\n\nMood: quiet, cinematic, precise, warm, confident. Use the theatre vocabulary everywhere, plainly: parts, cast, director, notes, reel, callbacks, opening night, call time, call sheet. Specific numbers only: 10 hours a week, 5–8 weeks, 62% callbacks, $89.\n\n## Brand\n\n- **Palette (eight colours; tints only via `color-mix()`, registered in `scripts/templates.config.ts`):** house `#F4F4F1` (page), white `#FFFFFF` (windows, glass), ink `#0D0D10` (text, the dark house, the arch), night `#1A1A1F` (cards, curtains), graphite `#66666E` (muted text), cue `#5B3CF0` (the one accent on light: the wave, numbers, focus, errors), cue light `#AE9CFF` (the accent on dark), tape `#FFC547` (rare: spike tape, the ghost light, the highlighter; never text).\n- **Type (`next/font/google`, distinct variables):** Google Sans Flex with the `ROND` axis (`--font-us-flex`, `font-variation-settings: \"ROND\" 70`) for display (light 340–400, tracking −0.04 to −0.05em) and body; Google Sans Code (`--font-us-code`, `preload: false`) for 11–12px uppercase labels; Literata italic (`--font-us-literata`, `preload: false`) for the director's notes and serif wordmarks.\n- **Logo:** a rounded-top arch with the curtain parted at its foot, `M6 46V22C6 12.06 14.06 4 24 4s18 8.06 18 18v24H28.6L24 30.5 19.4 46H6Z` in a 48 viewBox, plus the lowercase wordmark \"understudy\" (weight 520, −0.045em). Ship `brand/Logo.tsx` (Mark + lockup), `icon.svg` and a 1200×630 `opengraph-image.tsx`.\n- **Signature details:** the arch is the door; a purple light slit where the curtains part; spike-tape X/T/L floor marks; the ghost light; a cue dot (a small face in a cue ring) that pops at the end of each held line; a live \"Rehearsing now 1,284\" readout; a call sheet with a stable `US-XXXX` reference and a \"Cast\" stamp.\n- **Art:** no photography is required. Draw the studio from above in SVG (`brand/StageFloor.tsx`: stage with apron and wings, three blocks of seat rows as dashed arcs around a centre, aisles, spike marks, the ghost light), pale in the hero and lit in the finale. Part cards show a line-drawn prop (chart, braces, cursor, nib, megaphone, gear, flask, compass, bubble, stack) standing in a spotlight that follows the pointer. Eighteen invented companies are wordmarks made of a glyph and one of six type treatments (bold, light, caps, mono, serif, round). `Picture` swaps in optional `part-<id>` photos.\n\n## Page\n\n1. **Top bar** (fixed): lockup left, live readout right. Ink flips to house over dark scenes.\n2. **Hero** (`#top`, held for 340svh, sticky stage): the pale floor under a mist; eyebrow \"The rehearsal studio for first jobs\"; the headline \"Your first job, / rehearsed.\" plus a cue dot; the arch under it (width `clamp(76px, 7.2vw, 112px)`, height ×1.1667, centred at `50% + clamp(84px, 12svh, 130px)`), its curtain breathing, rings of cue leaving its foot, and a \"Scroll to enter\" nudge. Inside the same stage, the **dark house** is a full-screen ink layer whose `clip-path` is the arch: curtains (night with fold stripes), a light slit, then \"3,400+ / companies casting from our studio.\" over two rows of companies.\n3. **Parts** (`#parts`, ink, one screen): \"We cast you in the parts companies are hiring for.\" with chips (Popular, Product, Engineering, Data, Design, Growth, Operations; a pill glides between them as a `clip-path`, and ←/→ move it) and an **endless ring** of ten tall cards that drifts right to left (−36 px/s) in a gentle arc and loops with no seam: `loopX(i, n, slot, offset) = wrap(i·slot + offset + slot, n·slot) − slot`; short filtered sets repeat (`repsFor`) with the repeats hidden from readers; each card lifts and turns as it comes round the edges (`y = 34·d²`, `rotate = 5°·d`, `scale = 1 − 0.05·d²`, radius never under 560px). The page scroll pulls the ring along (0.32 px per px); drag it 1:1 and fling it (eased back with a frame-rate-independent `coast`); hover glides it to a stop; pause, keyboard focus or an open card brake it hard; previous / next (and ←/→ on the region) glide one card; a focused card glides fully into view; a drag never opens a card. One rAF loop writes the transforms and sleeps when still or off screen. Under reduced motion (and without JS) it's a plain row that scrolls natively. Each card shows: tag (Hot / Rising / New), \"{weeks}-week run · {company}\", title, tools, and a \"Brief →\" key. The card turns over (rotateY, inert faces) to the brief, three things you ship, and \"Rehearse this part\", which opens Start with that part chosen. Parts: AI Engineer (Quillo), Product Analyst (Parcelpost), Product Manager (fernway), Product Designer (Brightloop), Growth Marketer (sundry), Data Scientist (Tidewell), Frontend Engineer (LUMEN GAMES), Content Designer (Halcyon Bank), Operations Analyst (Saltbox), Customer Success Lead (Ledgerly).\n4. **Rehearsal** (`#rehearsal`): a dark bowl (the parts' black ends in an ellipse) that flattens with the scroll; the held line \"Learn the part by playing it.\" (sticky, behind a house fade); four scenes strung on one thread: a lead-in path from the line to a rail on the left, a node beside each scene. Scenes: \"Join a company that runs like a *real one.*\" (a lot of eight company buildings and a brief card: \"31% of carts stall at the address step. Find out why.\"), \"Run lines with your *cast.*\" (a team chat whose messages arrive one by one and a standup checklist), \"Get notes from your *director.*\" (a readout with highlighted findings, three margin notes in Literata, \"Approved\"), \"Walk out with a *reel.*\" (Maya Ortiz, Product Analyst · Level 7, −18% drop-off, 4 fixes, 9 notes counting up). Each window has a three-light bar and a mono title.\n5. **Casting** (`#casting`, held for 280svh): \"You rehearse. We *cast.*\", the reel in the middle of a 1200×600 stage, ten companies placed five each side; for each, a curve draws from the reel and then the company pops in; a \"Callbacks\" odometer rolls 00 → 10; a halo rings the reel when all ten are in.\n6. **Contract** (`#pricing`, held for 250svh): \"Built to get you [contract] *cast.*\": a cue wave runs through the letters, a small two-sheet contract scales out of the gap, a signature draws on it, a cue seal stamps; then \"Opening night is free. Then $89 a month.\", the CTA \"Book your first call time\" (magnetic) and \"Ask a question first\", and three facts (10 h a week · 5–8 weeks to a reel · 62% got a callback).\n7. **Finale** (the footer, held for 200svh): the floor pale, then the lit floor revealed by a `clip-path: circle()` growing from the ghost light; \"House lights up. *Take the stage.*\"; a glass ring whose text \"START · TAKE THE STAGE ·\" turns on a `textPath` around an ink core with the mark: the whole ring is the Start key. Legal links and \"a fictional brand for a landing-page template\".\n8. **Dock** (fixed, bottom centre, glass that turns dark over dark scenes): Start (ink, cue on dark, sheen on hover, a halo pulse), menu (two bars morph to ×), \"?\" (Ask). A hairline across it fills with page progress. Any `[data-us-start]`, `[data-us-ask]` or `[data-us-go]` elsewhere opens the matching sheet or scrolls. Sheets open out of the dock with a `clip-path`, hold the page still (Lenis stops), trap focus, close on Escape or the scrim, and give focus back.\n   - **Menu:** five scenes, a pill that slides to the current one.\n   - **Start:** floating-label name, email and part select. Rules in `forms/audition.ts` (tested): name 1–60, a valid email, a listed part. Invalid fields shake once, their messages slide down, focus goes to the first error. Submit rolls the label out to bouncing dots for 950ms, then the form clips away and a call sheet slides up: \"You're on the call sheet, {first name}.\", ref, part, company, first call (the next weekday at 09:00), a \"Cast\" stamp; \"Book another part\" resets. No network request.\n   - **Ask:** the FAQ is a question you type. `ask/answer.ts` (tested) scores keyword hits (plural-tolerant) across eight answers (price, experience, hours, real companies, getting hired, cast, director, reel) with a polite fallback; a typing beat of 750ms; suggestion chips.\n\n## The mechanic: one scene clock\n\nPure, tested maths in `stage/` and `motion/math.ts`:\n\n- `pinProgress(y, top, height, vh) = clamp((y − top) / (height − vh))`, `flowProgress(y, top, height, vh, line)`, and `toneAt(boxes, y, vh, probe)` (a scene may turn dark part-way via `data-dark-from`; the hero does at 0.42).\n- `archInset(t, vw, vh, rest)`: insets that leave exactly the resting arch at `t = 0` and nothing at `t = 1`, eased by `t³` (the camera accelerates into the opening); top radius `w / 2 → 0`. `t = range(p, 0.04, 0.52)`.\n- `lensPlace(x, half, bend)`: `y = −46·d²·bend`, `rotate = −9°·d·bend`, `scale = 1 − 0.16·d²·bend`, `d = x / half` clamped to ±1.4 (the rows bow like the inside of a cylinder). `rowLayout(count, slot, offset)` wraps a looping row; rows drift 26 px/s in opposite directions.\n- `callbacksAt(p, n)` and `digitsOf(n, width)` for the casting slice and the odometer.\n\n`stage/ScrollDirector.tsx` (the only scroll owner) measures scenes on resize and, in one rAF, writes `--p` on every `[data-scene]`; it writes only the arch `clip-path`, the lens transforms (in a rAF loop that runs only while the house is open and on screen), the callbacks' `data-on` and the odometer digits itself. Everything else is CSS reading `--p` through `clamp()`. **The wave:** letters carry `--i`, the line carries `--n`; `w = wp·(n + 10) − 5`, `d = w − i`, `hot = clamp(0, 1 − max(d, −d) / 3.5, 1)`, `lit = clamp(0, d / 2.5, 1)`, colour = `color-mix(in oklab, cue hot%, color-mix(ink lit%, dim))`. A sticky line can't be measured, so a measuring strip feeds its progress to it (`data-feeds` → `--fed`).\n\n## Motion\n\n- **Identity:** technical / cinematic. The verbs are **push in** and **cue**. The signature moment is the push through the proscenium.\n- **Tokens in `@theme`:** ease-out `cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.715, 0.205, 0.99)`, expo `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`, in-out `cubic-bezier(0.625, 0.05, 0, 1)`, spring `linear(0, 0.35 8%, 0.82 20%, 1.06 30%, 1.02 42%, 0.99 58%, 1)`, pop `cubic-bezier(0.3, 1.45, 0.45, 1)`; durations 90 / 180 / 420 / 800 / 1200ms; shift 28px; stagger 70ms.\n- **Load (CSS keyframes, before hydration, using `translate`/`scale` so scroll transforms never collide):** floor settles from 1.06 at 0ms, logo drops at 150ms, eyebrow slides at 200ms, headline lines rise through masks at 260 and 380ms, dock rises at 420ms, the arch rises at 640ms, the cue dot pops at 1s, rings start at 1.1s, the curtain breathes from 1.6s, the nudge appears at 1.9s.\n- **Reveals (one shared IntersectionObserver flips `data-in`; observe the unclipped parent of masks and wipes):** mask-rise for titles, rise for body, slide for labels, pop-scale for nodes, clip-path wipe with an inner 1.08 → 1 settle for windows, stroke-draw for props and the thread, count-up for stats. Each window then plays its scene (buildings pop, the brief slides in, messages arrive, ticks draw, the highlighter wipes, notes slide in, the stamp lands).\n- **States:** buttons fill-wipe from the cursor side and roll their label (the duplicate label is `content: attr(data-text) / \"\"` so screen readers hear it once), press to .97 with a spring release; cards lift 8px with a pointer spotlight and dim their siblings; windows lift and glow; links draw their underline; inputs tighten their focus ring; the ring scales and its core turns cue.\n- **Ambient (paused off screen and in hidden tabs):** the curtain's breath and the rings, the lens rows, the live readout, the dock halo, the lot pin, the turning ring.\n- **Smooth scroll:** Lenis (`lerp 0.105`) on fine pointers with motion allowed, dynamically imported; it also answers `us:goto` and `us:lock`.\n- **Reduced motion and no JS:** every held, hidden or clipped state lives inside `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (scripting: enabled)`, so the default CSS is the finished page: the headline with the mark, the dark house as a normal section with straight rows, parts scrolling natively with snap, every line lit, the contract signed, the finale lit. Add a `<noscript>` reveal override, and give Start links an email fallback.\n\n## Quality bar\n\n- Animate only `transform`, `translate`, `scale`, `opacity`, `clip-path` and stroke offsets. CLS 0. No horizontal overflow at 375px. Base resets wrapped in `:where()` so component classes always win.\n- One h1, landmarks, real buttons and links, AA contrast, visible focus rings, dialog titles as labelled paragraphs (not headings), live regions only for the form status and the Ask log.\n- Structure: `brand/`, `content/` (typed data + tests), `stage/` (maths + tests, the director), `motion/` (math + tests, Reveal, WaveLine, SmoothScroll, Magnetic, CountUp, LiveCount), `parts/` (ring maths + tests, the ring hook, the cards), `rehearsal/`, `sections/`, `dock/`, `forms/` (rules + tests), `ask/` (matcher + tests), `assets.tsx`.\n- Playwright covers the push-in (clip-path opens, chrome turns dark, rows drift), the ring (drift, seamless loop under repeated flings, hover stop, pause, next, no click after a drag), refilled short sets, chip filtering and arrow keys, the card flip and the prefilled Start, validation, focus, success with no request, reset and focus return, Ask, the menu, reveals and the thread, callbacks and the odometer, the contract's seal, hover and press, reduced motion (static, nothing hidden), no-JS, and the phone dock and menu.\n";

